104
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
In this podcast we're going halfway around the world to Australia,

105
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
where we're going to be speaking with the chair

106
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
of the National Youth Council of Scouts Australia.

107
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
I had a great time talking with Chris Nielsen

108
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
and I can't wait for you to hear the interview.

109
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
It's going to take up the remainder of the podcast,

110
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
so let's get started, shall we?

111
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
The Scout movement is forming a personal tie

112
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
between the different foreign countries.

113
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,000
A living force, a great brotherhood of service.

114
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
a joyous work.

115
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Scouts Australia established a National Youth Council

116
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
about a dozen years ago, in 2001,,

117
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
and they did this to engage youth members

118
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:02,000
in advising the organization on the relevance of their programs.

119
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
I mean, what a great idea:

120
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
Actually talk to the people who are on the receiving end of the programs.

121
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Now there are 25 members on the council

122
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
from all branches of Scouts Australia,

123
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
aged between 13 and 25,

124
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
and they each serve a three-year term.

125
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
The council is separated into four working patrols

126
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:28,000
and each researches and writes recommendations on their own topics.

127
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
The only non-youth member of the council is the advisor,

128
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,000
who does really not have any voting rights or anything like that.

129
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,000
The chair of this National Youth Council

130
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:44,000
is a full member of the Scouts Australia National Council

131
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
and has voting rights on the National Operations Committee

132
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
and the National Executive Committee.

133
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:55,000
I'm really pleased that the current National Youth Council chair

134
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
is able to join us on the Scoutmaster podcast today.

135
00:08:58,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Hello, Chris.

136
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Hi,

137
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
Chris Nielsen is joining us from Melbourne,

138
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Is that right?

139
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,000
Yes, Melbourne.

140
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:05,000
Oh, okay,

141
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Melbourne.

142
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Yeah,

143
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,000
You're going to have to correct my pronunciation as we go along,

144
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:15,000
because Australian Scouting would be very familiar to us,

145
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
I think in the United States.

146
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:22,000
But let's just go through your career in Scouting a little bit, Chris.

147
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
You started out at the age of six,

148
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Is that correct?

149
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
About a week before I turned six, because that's our minimum age.

150
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Uh-huh,

151
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,000
And what are Scouts called at that level? in Scouts Australia

152
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,000
They're called joeys, which is the word for baby kangaroos.

153
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Okay,

154
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:41,000
So they're really cute.

155
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
They stand in a circle and put their hands up like little paws

156
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:46,000
and go hop, help other people.

157
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,000
It's adorable,

158
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Oh, cool.

159
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Help other people hop.

160
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Oh, I like that.

161
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
The group of joeys is called a mob,

162
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:53,000
Is that right?

163
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Uh-huh,

164
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:55,000
I love that.

165
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Just like a group of kangaroos,

166
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
Well, just like a group of six-year-olds.

167
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,000
Yeah,

168
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:00,000
You know,

169
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,000
It's kind of like a mob,

170
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:09,000
And that was at what was then called the sixth ringwood scout

171
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,000
group.

172
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Yep,

173
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,000
Okay,

174
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
But then we merged with another group when I was in Cubs

175
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
and we're now called First Maroondah.

176
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Explain to us a little bit.

177
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
We're not familiar with the group concept here.

178
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:26,000
So a group is the group is all of the sections that meet

179
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:27,000
there.

180
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
So we have joeys, Cubs, Scouts, Venturers and Rovers.

181
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,000
Not every group would have all five, but my group does,

182
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:37,000
And so the group is everyone who meets that hall.

183
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:42,000
and you know we have group counsel to work things out.

184
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
And then we're also within a district in Victoria.

185
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Some of the different states in Australia work things out

186
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
differently, but in Victoria the groups are arranged

187
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
into districts and the districts are arranged

188
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:53,000
into regions

189
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,000
And then there's the whole Victorian branch.

190
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
It's girls and boys in the same scouting program.

191
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Yes,

192
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
All the way from six.

193
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
We also have guides, which is like your girl Scouts,

194
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
but that is a separate movement.

195
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Scouts itself is boys and girls.

196
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
You're in a Rover crew,

197
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:11,000
Is that right?

198
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,000
Yes,

199
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
Describe what a Rover crew is, because that's kind of

200
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
foreign to us. in the United States,

201
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
Rover crews are 18 to 25 year old Scouts,

202
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
So it's a little bit like the upper end of your

203
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
Venturer section.

204
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
It's relatively similar to all of the other sections,

205
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:31,000
except given that we're all adults,

206
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,000
So we can all drive ourselves places and we can all

207
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
do more adult things and give our own permission

208
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,000
and stuff like that.

209
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Rovers is self governing,

210
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:45,000
So it means that Rovers make their own decisions.

211
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:46,000
We don't have leaders,

212
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
We have advisors

213
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,000
And there is, you know, a region Rover Council

214
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
and a branch or so Victoria wide Rover Council

215
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,000
And they make decisions about how Rovers is going to run.

216
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
And there's also a National Rover Council.

217
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,000
As the National Youth Council,

218
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
I work very closely with the National Rover Council.

219
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
What kind of things do Rovers do?

220
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
Do you go camping?

221
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
You go hiking and things like that.

222
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,000
We do all of that.

223
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:15,000
We meet sort of weekly and do different activities.

224
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:19,000
You know, last week my Rover crew was doing New Age night

225
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
And we sat there doing like tarot readings

226
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
and having heaps of fun playing with the things

227
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
and just making up funny stories about them.

228
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
You know we go and do sort of adventurous things.

229
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
My crew went ab-sailing recently.

230
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
We go camping.

231
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Our motto is service in the Rover crew,

232
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
in the Rover section as well.

233
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
So my Rover crew runs a competition hike

234
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
for scouts in our district.

235
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
And you're also working with Cubs.

236
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Yes,

237
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:47,000
I'm a cub leader.

238
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
My cub name is Matka, which is the seal.

239
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
No one's ever heard of it. but you know

240
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,000
I actually started working with Cubs

241
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
back when I was about 16 or 17

242
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
because I had to do 20 hours of service

243
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
to get my Queen Scout Award.

244
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
It's the highest award we can get before you turn 18..

245
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
And then I just kind of never left.

246
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:07,000
I really enjoyed it.

247
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
You're currently at the University of Melbourne.

248
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,000
Yes,

249
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
I'm studying a master of environment.

250
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
One thing I saw in your bio that I found very interesting:

251
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,000
you took a gap year.

252
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:18,000
Yeah,

253
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
It was a break year between high school

254
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
and before you went to university.

255
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:23,000
What did you do with that year?

256
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
I went to China as a volunteer

257
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
and I taught conversational English for four months,

258
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
So I was just 18.

259
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
And so it was challenging but a lot of fun.

260
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
And then we traveled China for a month afterwards

261
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
and then, you know, did a little bit of Europe

262
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
and came home ready to start uni.

263
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
That must have been amazing.

264
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:43,000
It was fantastic.

265
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:46,000
I mean, it was very challenging at times,

266
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:49,000
Very much sometimes. you know,

267
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
we talk about personal development a lot in Scouts

268
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
and that was a bit of a personal development moment

269
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:55,000
for me a couple of times.

270
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
You know, being alone in a foreign country

271
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
where you only speak a little bit of the language,

272
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
it was very difficult at times,

273
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
but it was also a lot of fun

274
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,000
and I really enjoyed learning more Chinese language

275
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
and meeting all the people

276
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
and getting to know their culture and so on a lot more.

277
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:13,000
And it's a beautiful country.

278
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
I'm sure that your participation in Scouts-

279
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
you called on a lot of things

280
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:19,000
that you learned along the way

281
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
as you went through that gap here,

282
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
All of the skills we learned in Scouting,

283
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
like you know, your initiative

284
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
and being self-reliant and facing challenges

285
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,000
and knowing that you can.

286
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:32,000
Okay, it's really difficult now,

287
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:33,000
but I'm sure I'll get through it

288
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
and I'll enjoy this later.

289
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Yeah, I definitely drew on all of those skills.

290
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Tell me about how you got involved with that

291
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
Poll National Youth Council.

292
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
We have a rotating membership,

293
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:47,000
which means we have about eight people leave each year

294
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
and then we select eight new ones

295
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:50,000
and then you're on for three years.

296
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:51,000
I applied three times.

297
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
I applied once when I was 14 and I didn't get on,

298
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
and then I applied in 2009

299
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,000
when I got back from China, and I didn't get on.

300
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,000
but I applied again at the start of 2010

301
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:02,000
and I got on, which was very exciting.

302
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
So, as I'm sure you can understand,

303
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
only taking eight people a year

304
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:10,000
and we want to cover all of Australia

305
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
and we also want to make sure that we have representation

306
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
of the different sections and so on.

307
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
Now that I'm selecting people,

308
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
we've got so many great applicants,

309
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
so it is very much a juggling act,

310
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
but yeah, so I got on in 2010,

311
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:25,000
which was very exciting.

312
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
Then you've had several different positions there

313
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
over the past couple of years.

314
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Yes, I was appointed minute secretary

315
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
in 2010 in my first year,

316
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:35,000
which isn't that exciting.

317
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:38,000
I just took minutes.

318
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:42,000
Yes, everybody needs somebody to write down the minutes.

319
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:47,000
Then in 2011, in March, I was elected as the vice chair

320
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:52,000
and then in March of 2012, I was elected chair,

321
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:53,000
which is a two year position.

322
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
so I've got a little bit under one year left.

323
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:59,000
so I'm no longer in a patrol

324
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
because I'm a little bit busy,

325
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
but I'm sort of in charge of all four patrols-

326
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
A little bit busy, I think,

327
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
is the understatement.

328
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,000
You've been doing quite a bit of travelling, haven't you?

329
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:15,000
Yeah, way back in 2010,

330
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
when I applied to be on the National Youth Council,

331
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
I also, a little bit after that,

332
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:22,000
applied to be one of Australia's observers

333
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
to go to the World Youth Forum,

334
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:25,000
which was in Brazil.

335
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,000
so I was selected as one of our three observers.

336
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
so I headed over to Brazil

337
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
and that was fantastic.

338
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
Last year it was the Queen's Jubilee

339
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
and we're still a Commonwealth country,

340
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:42,000
so they decided to hold a Commonwealth Youth Forum

341
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
to celebrate the Queen's Jubilee.

342
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
and, again, I just applied

343
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,000
to be one of the two people that we sent

344
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,000
and I was selected, which I was quite surprised about.

345
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:56,000
so I got to go over and go to the Commonwealth Youth Forum

346
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:00,000
and also got to march in the English Queen Scout Parade

347
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:03,000
and we got to meet the Queen and Bear Grills,

348
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
which was very, very exciting,

349
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:06,000
Oh my

350
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
Yeah. I mean like meet the Queen.

351
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
we were within like a metre of the Queen

352
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
but we actually shook Bear Grills' hands,

353
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
so that was very exciting.

354
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
Just in November last year

355
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
it was the Asia-Pacific region

356
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
Scout Youth Forum and Conference

357
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
and, as I'm now in the National Youth Council chair,

358
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
I was one of Australia's two delegates-

359
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:26,000
didn't get enough sleep

360
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
because I joined the recommendations committee,

361
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:31,000
so we went to bed at like 5am one night

362
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
because we were fixing up all the recommendations

363
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:34,000
for the next day.

364
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
but it was really, really good to be involved

365
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
in the organisation a little bit.

366
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,000
Why should there be an organised youth voice in Scouting?

367
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:45,000
Part of it is that Scouting is about young people,

368
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
and young people know what they want,

369
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
and young people are different.

370
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
Even the other day I was at Cubs

371
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
and I was testing one of my Cubs

372
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
on her Traveller Level 1 badge,

373
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
and so one of the things she had to do

374
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
was tell me some of the good and bad bits

375
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
about four different kinds of transport,

376
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:05,000
and how she described trains and buses,

377
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,000
which I take every day,

378
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
was just completely different

379
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
to how I would describe trains and buses.

380
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,000
One of the bad bits about a bus

381
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
was that it wasn't as long as a train.

382
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:14,000
I think I understand Cubs,

383
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
but Cubs think in a very different way

384
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:17,000
to how I do.

385
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
As a rover, I probably think quite differently

386
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
to how some of the other members

387
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
of the national team and so on think.

388
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
so it's really important to have

389
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
young people saying what they want.

390
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
if we want to keep young people,

391
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
we need to make sure we change with the times.

392
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
things have changed so much

393
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
in the last couple of decades

394
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
with technology and all this kind of stuff.

395
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
I don't know about America,

396
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
but in Australia there's a lot more options

397
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:44,000
for young people as well.

398
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
Lots of schools are now implementing programs

399
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
that are a little bit more like Scouts,

400
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and there's so many different hobbies

401
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:51,000
that people can join

402
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
and all these different sports and so on,

403
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:54,000
and kids are getting busy

404
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:55,000
because they're doing more.

405
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
so if we want to sort of

406
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:58,000
stay relevant to young people,

407
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
we need to make sure that young people are involved

408
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
in telling us what relevant is.

409
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
Let me occupy the position

410
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
of being the devil's advocate for a moment.

411
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
I'm in my mid-fifties.

412
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
I would probably be a little frightened

413
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:13,000
of youth having an active,

414
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
influential voice,

415
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
because I think what my worry would be

416
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
is that they would try to,

417
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,000
you know, topple over all of the traditions

418
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
and you know they would stop going camping

419
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
and start playing video games,

420
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
or I don't really get the sense that

421
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
that is what happens. though

422
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
Generally, I think you find that

423
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
young people value a lot of the same things

424
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,000
that a lot of the older leaders

425
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
and so on value, and it is

426
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
it's the camping in the outdoors

427
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
and the trying new things,

428
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
and young people value that just as much.

429
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:50,000
Sometimes they want to try different new things

430
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:53,000
or they want to change things a little bit.

431
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
but you know we're definitely still

432
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
going camping and the National Youth Council.

433
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
we don't often write recommendations

434
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
about changing

435
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
huge things, like

436
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
let's stop going camping

437
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
or something like that, because

438
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
we all understand that that's what

439
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
scouting is like. we've all been in scouting

440
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
and we all love that aspect of scouting.

441
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
We often write things about

442
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
issues people might have, like

443
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
bullying or something like that.

444
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
you know new badges and we talk about

445
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
maybe new programs that could be introduced,

446
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
but we often very little. we should throw this out.

447
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
we should introduce this as well.

448
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
this would be great. We're utilized quite a lot

449
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
by the National team

450
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
in terms of getting feedback

451
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
on ideas that they're having.

452
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
so they kind of go: hey,

453
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
we're thinking about doing this,

454
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
do you reckon it sounds any good?

455
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
or we're thinking about doing this,

456
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
give us some ideas on how that would work.

457
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
we're kind of the research body for Nationals,

458
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
so they'll be

459
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
asking us to go research

460
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
what we're doing, maybe what other scouting countries are doing.

461
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
so we'll sit there on the BSA website

462
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
and look up what they're doing

463
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
to deal with a similar issue, say

464
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
bullying. okay, we'll house life saving

465
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
dealing with this, house

466
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
England dealing with this in scouts,

467
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
all these kinds of things.

468
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
so we do quite a wide variety of topics.

469
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
we're not usually telling people to throw out

470
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
things that are

471
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
part of scouting, because we do value a lot of

472
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
the things that are in scouting.

473
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
sometimes we're talking about introducing new things,

474
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
just changing things to make them

475
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
a little bit more modern or relevant.

476
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
what's the dynamic between

477
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
the

478
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
Youth Council and your adult counterparts?

479
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
you have the Youth Council

480
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
and it's working cooperatively

481
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
with the Executive

482
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
Board of Scouts Australia,

483
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
and am I right in

484
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
thinking that it's kind of a parallel

485
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
to a similar group of adults?

486
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
well, we definitely work

487
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
very closely with the National team

488
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
and all of the National bodies.

489
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
I sit on a lot of them

490
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
as the chair of the National Youth Council.

491
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
they come to our conferences

492
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
but all of the people

493
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
involved in decision making

494
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
are very, very open to what the National Youth Council

495
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
has to say and we have

496
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
definitely made decisions

497
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
just in the time I've been chair

498
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
at a National level where some of the adults

499
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
in the room have gone: oh, I don't like this idea.

500
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
but the National Youth Council says

501
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
that the young people do, so we'll do it anyway.

502
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
I noted that one of the projects

503
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
that you've worked on was

504
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
in dealing with the uniform.

505
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
so Scouts Australia changed its uniform

506
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
back in about 2002

507
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
or 2003. we sort of decided

508
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
maybe it was time for a little bit

509
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
of an update, just to see

510
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
that it was okay. and you know we weren't going to say

511
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
we'll scrap it and put in a new

512
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
uniform again, but we wanted to say:

513
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
you know, is there anything that irritates you

514
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
a little bit that we could tweak a bit?

515
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
we run a survey of both adults and youth

516
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
scouting. this survey had the most responses

517
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
we'd ever had. we were so excited, although it was

518
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
a lot of work, partly because you know the magic word

519
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
uniform. I don't know if it happens

520
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
for you guys as well, but the instant you ask

521
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
about something that's a little bit

522
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
divisive than everyone wants to have

523
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
their say: oh, yes, yes, we're very

524
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
familiar with that. yes,

525
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
so the the uniform.

526
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
this survey was

527
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
really really great response rate. and

528
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
then we ran a follow-up survey as well,

